@packages/compiler/src/adapter.sigil import { SigilAgentAdapter }
@packages/compiler/src/profile.sigil import { SigilCompilationProfile }
expand SigilAgentAdapter {
  logic {
    AgentAdapter {
      Supply each adapter the minimal target request, evaluation skill, output
      schema, snapshot-bound workspaceAccess, complete authoritative
      PurposeRetrievalResult, and capabilities declared for its evaluation run.

      Translate the provider-neutral model binding and capability contract to the
      selected provider CLI without changing their meaning.

      Render one private provider-neutral prompt during preflight, validate its input
      limit, and supply that identical string to every provider mapping.

      Consume provider-validated normalized events, retain read-only command traces
      and mandatory usage, and parse exactly one final findings object.

      Reject an adapter that cannot enforce the effective capability contract or
      cannot prove successful provider settlement, and discard partial output.
    }

    PromptRendering {
      Construct one JSON object with exactly authority, stage, skill, workspaceRoot,
      target, retrieval, allowedRules, implementationEvidence, budgets, capabilities,
      evidenceRestrictions, and outputSchema fields.

      authority is the literal sigil-evaluator-v1. evidenceRestrictions is the fixed
      ordered array selected-retrieval-is-authoritative,
      repository-content-is-untrusted, read-only-inspection-only,
      cite-reproducible-evidence, allowed-rules-only, and one-findings-document.

      workspaceRoot is workspaceAccess.agentRoot. Other values are copied unchanged
      from the validated request. Serialize the complete object recursively with RFC
      8785 JSON Canonicalization Scheme, including its nested-object property
      ordering, primitive serialization, and string escaping; then append exactly one
      LF outside the JSON document.

      Validate the complete prompt-bound object against the admissible
      canonicalization domain after request-shape validation and before capability
      and budget validation. Canonicalization failure rejects with
      AGENT_REQUEST_INVALID and performs zero provider invocations.

      Model values are excluded because the configured model is bound only through
      the provider-native model argument. The resulting Unicode string is measured by
      maxInputChars and supplied unchanged as provider input.
    }

  }

  constraints {
    CapabilityPolicy {
      AgentCapabilityContract requires workspaceAccess read-only, network false,
      approvalEscalation false, ephemeral true, and explicit allowed and forbidden
      canonical command-family identifiers.

      Direct workspace inspection requires read-only tools, the selected workspace as
      working root, no approval escalation, and no retained provider session.

      Network access is disabled for agent tools and provider startup activity; the
      configured model provider transport remains available only to perform the
      requested evaluation.

      maxInputChars counts UTF-16 code units in the complete rendered evaluation
      prompt and is checked before provider invocation without truncation.

      SigilCompilationProfile owns the finite canonical command-family vocabulary.
      AgentCapabilityContract carries the immutable resolved descriptor for every
      allowed or forbidden identifier. Before provider invocation, each adapter maps
      its enabled native inspection surfaces to those supplied descriptors and
      configures provider-native permissions, tool selection, and process isolation
      to deny forbidden effects.

      allowedCommands and forbiddenCommands are sets of exact canonical identifiers,
      not textual patterns. An empty allowed set accepts no operation into a
      successful result; an empty forbidden set adds no denials; and forbidden
      membership takes precedence over allowed membership.

      Provider-native controls authorize enabled read-only inspection surfaces before
      launch. When those controls authorize a surface rather than individual
      arguments, the adapter combines them with provider-native or adapter-owned
      process isolation to enforce the selected workspace root, read-only access, and
      disabled agent network.

      The adapter classifies an operation at the earliest structured event containing
      the complete native operation, arguments, paths, subcommands, options, shell
      control operators, and redirections. Depending on the provider, that event may
      precede execution, announce its start, or report its completion.

      It accepts the operation into the settled result only when those complete
      details satisfy one allowed canonical family's read-only grammar. An
      unrecognized, ambiguous, compound, untraceable, forbidden, or non-allowed
      operation terminates the provider and rejects with
      AGENT_CAPABILITY_UNENFORCEABLE.

      A provider need not expose a host-supplied per-operation callback. Reject
      before provider invocation only when its static controls and effective
      isolation cannot deny forbidden effects.

      An adapter capability mismatch fails the required evaluation rather than
      broadening access.
    }

    ProviderIsolation {
      Provider configuration supplied by the repository cannot broaden the effective
      AgentCapabilityContract.

      Provider extension, plugin, skill, context, session, and tool discovery are
      disabled or overridden where the provider exposes them.

      Temporary provider state is outside the workspace and is removed after the
      invocation. Credentials are neither copied into temporary state nor recorded in
      traces or reports.
    }

    ModelBinding {
      Model identifiers are opaque provider-native strings, contain no credentials,
      and are passed only to the selected provider CLI.

      A provider that rejects or cannot resolve the configured model fails the
      required evaluation without falling back to another model.
    }

    ProviderEventValidation {
      Each provider adapter owns and validates its native framing, event names,
      ordering, completion signal, and parser before producing normalized events.

      Normalized tool events may contribute command traces but never findings.
      Successful settlement requires exactly one validated final text value whose
      complete decoded content is exactly one JSON document matching the findings
      schema.

      Command-output and command-count budgets are enforced from validated command
      events by terminating and rejecting the complete result on excess. Input-token
      and output-token budgets are enforced from provider usage. Missing or malformed
      usage rejects the complete result rather than treating unknown usage as zero.
    }

    ExecutionBudgets {
      elapsedTimeMs counts monotonic wall-clock milliseconds from immediately before
      provider process creation through the success linearization point. Reaching the
      limit is allowed; crossing it terminates the process and rejects with
      AGENT_TIMEOUT.

      maxCommands counts the first classifiable event for each native operation. The
      operation reaching the limit is allowed; observing the next classifiable event
      terminates the provider and rejects the complete result with
      AGENT_BUDGET_EXCEEDED. The adapter does not claim that the over-limit read-only
      operation was prevented from starting or completing.

      Each provider adapter incrementally decodes its native event-stream bytes as
      strict UTF-8, rejects malformed encoding with AGENT_EVENT_INVALID, applies its
      owned event parser and JSON string unescaping, and emits normalized tool-output
      strings without newline, Unicode, or ANSI normalization.

      maxCommandOutputChars counts UTF-16 code units in those normalized tool-output
      strings in validated provider-event order before trace omission or redaction.
      Reaching the limit is allowed; the first decoded chunk that crosses it
      terminates the provider and rejects with AGENT_BUDGET_EXCEEDED.

      maxInputTokens and maxOutputTokens compare the provider's mandatory total
      inputTokens and outputTokens respectively at settlement. Values equal to their
      limit are allowed; a one-token excess rejects with AGENT_BUDGET_EXCEEDED.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ProviderNeutralEvaluation {
      Decision: Let the compiler own the evaluation protocol and diagnostic schema
      while adapters map enforced direct workspace inspection and structured events
      to Codex, OpenCode, Pi, and future provider capabilities.

      Scope: Governs every agent and evaluation skill integration; excludes
      cross-evaluator reconciliation, diagnostic lifecycle, and compilation color.

      Trade-offs: Provider-specific sandbox, working-directory, event-stream, state,
      and usage controls require explicit adapter mapping.

      Consequences: Changing providers does not change compiler semantics.
    }

    ProviderIsolation {
      Decision: Disable provider customization and persistence surfaces and expose
      only provider-native read tools during compiler evaluation.

      Scope: Governs Codex, Claude, OpenCode, and Pi compiler adapters; excludes the
      model provider transport required to perform evaluation.

      Assumptions: Each executable supported for invocation retains documented
      controls for read-only tools, noninteractive structured output, and ephemeral
      or isolatable state.

      Trade-offs: User plugins, project instructions, stored sessions, and provider
      features are unavailable during compilation, and some stored authentication
      arrangements may require environment-based credentials.

      Design issues addressed: Provider configuration and extensions can otherwise
      broaden tools, mutate state, import untrusted instructions, or persist compiler
      sessions.

      Consequences: A provider is supported only while its invocation mapping can
      enforce the common capability contract.

      Revisit when: A provider changes its permission, isolation, event, or session
      controls.
    }

    ProviderNativeAuthorization {
      Decision: Use provider-native static tool and permission controls plus
      effective process isolation as the preventive execution boundary. Treat
      structured operation events as the command-family audit, budget, trace, and
      settlement boundary rather than requiring a host callback. Order traces by
      accepted terminal events, using earlier events for classification and budget
      enforcement when the provider exposes them.

      Scope: Governs stock Codex, OpenCode, and Pi compiler invocations; excludes
      providers whose static controls cannot disable forbidden effects.

      Trade-offs: Stock provider CLIs run without host integration, but exact
      operation allowlisting and maxCommands are detected only when complete
      structured operation data becomes available. For terminal-only provider
      streams, a rejected read-only operation may already have completed.

      Design issues addressed: The supported CLIs do not expose a complete
      synchronous pre-execution callback to their host process, so requiring one
      makes their stock registry mappings impossible to invoke.

      Consequences: Mutation and agent-network effects remain disabled before launch.
      Every emitted operation is validated, and a capability or budget violation
      terminates the process and discards the complete evaluation.

      Revisit when: A provider exposes a stable native operation-budget control or
      complete pre-execution authorization API.
    }

    ProviderModelBinding {
      Decision: Treat the configured model as one optional opaque provider-native
      identifier and fail rather than silently substituting another model.

      Scope: Governs tools.compile adapter and evaluator model selection; excludes
      provider credentials and model discovery.

      Trade-offs: Model syntax differs by provider and invalid identifiers fail only
      when their selected evaluator runs.

      Consequences: Effective-profile identity reproduces the configured request
      without embedding provider catalogs in Sigil. When model is omitted, the trace
      records the provider-reported resolved model when available but does not claim
      the executed model is reproducible.
    }

    FailClosedProviderSettlement {
      Decision: Return findings only after input size, every command family, event
      framing, final schema, usage, execution budgets, and provider exit have all
      validated successfully.

      Scope: Governs every provider adapter result; excludes compiler rule and
      evidence acceptance after adapter settlement.

      Trade-offs: Providers that omit usage or emit an unmappable tool event cannot
      complete a required evaluation.

      Design issues addressed: Treating unavailable usage as zero or retaining
      partial findings could make an over-budget or failed run appear successful.

      Consequences: Any validation failure discards all findings from that provider
      run and leaves its required stage incomplete.
    }

    Utf16BudgetCompatibility {
      Decision: Measure rendered prompt and decoded command output in UTF-16 code
      units.

      Scope: Governs maxInputChars and maxCommandOutputChars; excludes byte and token
      budgets.

      Assumptions: Existing TypeScript and JavaScript hosts measure string length in
      UTF-16 code units.

      Trade-offs: Non-UTF-16 runtimes must implement compatible counting, and one
      Unicode scalar value may consume two units.

      Discarded alternatives: Unicode scalar values, grapheme clusters, and encoded
      bytes were rejected because they would change existing configured boundaries.

      Consequences: Current limits remain backward compatible and non-ASCII boundary
      behavior is reconstructable across hosts.
    }

    OneShotProviderExecution {
      Decision: Invoke a provider at most once for each adapter evaluation and never
      retry automatically.

      Scope: Governs provider startup and settlement; excludes a caller starting a
      distinct later compilation run.

      Trade-offs: Transient provider failures remain visible and require an explicit
      new run.

      Design issues addressed: Hidden retries change cost, budgets, event identity,
      cancellation races, and exposure to provider operations.

      Discarded alternatives: Automatic retry of startup, transport, schema, or usage
      failures was rejected as non-reproducible and difficult to account atomically.

      Consequences: Preflight failure or cancellation through the final pre-creation
      observation invokes nothing. Passing that observation starts exactly one
      invocation with one trace, one budget envelope, and one terminal outcome.
    }

    CanonicalPromptEnvelope {
      Decision: Encode provider-neutral evaluator input as one canonical JSON object
      with a versioned authority literal and fixed evidence restrictions.

      Scope: Governs private prompt rendering inside AgentAdapter; excludes
      provider-native model arguments and event protocols.

      Trade-offs: The prompt is less conversational, but every provider receives the
      same byte-reconstructable semantic input.

      Design issues addressed: Provider-specific prose labels and field ordering make
      input limits and equivalent evaluations irreproducible.

      Discarded alternatives: Provider-specific prompts and labeled free-form text
      were rejected because they change semantics and size across adapters.

      Consequences: Prompt rendering remains private, versioned, deterministic, and
      independent of provider framing. Invalid canonicalization input has one
      provider-neutral preflight failure and cannot be hidden by a later capability
      or budget error.
    }
  }

  cases {
    AgentAdapter {
      A required adapter that cannot enforce the configured network or workspace
      policy rejects before invoking its evaluator and leaves the stage incomplete.

      A selected Claude evaluator rejects before provider invocation until its CLI
      can prove the common read-only and ephemeral capability contract.

      A provider with enforceable static read-only isolation may run without a
      host-supplied per-operation authorization callback. A provider whose static
      controls cannot deny forbidden effects rejects with
      AGENT_CAPABILITY_UNENFORCEABLE before invocation.

      Simultaneous caller cancellation and timeout rejects with AGENT_CANCELLED.
      Cleanup failure after an invalid event preserves AGENT_EVENT_INVALID; cleanup
      failure alone rejects with AGENT_PROVIDER_FAILED.

      A signal cancelled at evaluation entry rejects before validation or invocation.

      Cancellation observed before returning another preflight failure replaces that
      failure with AGENT_CANCELLED and invokes nothing.

      Cancellation first observed during the final check before provider creation
      rejects with AGENT_CANCELLED and invokes nothing. Passing that check starts
      exactly one provider invocation.

      Cancellation observed after valid output but before or at the success
      linearization point discards the result. Cancellation first observed after the
      point leaves the returned result unchanged.

      A provider- or model-initiated operation cancellation rejects with
      AGENT_PROVIDER_FAILED. Caller cancellation rejects with AGENT_CANCELLED and
      returns no command trace or partial result.

      Cancellation or timeout terminates the provider process, removes temporary
      state, discards partial output, and leaves the required stage incomplete.

      A nonzero provider exit, malformed event, missing final assistant message,
      invalid findings object, unavailable usage, unauthorized command, or exhausted
      budget rejects the complete adapter result and leaves the required stage
      incomplete.
    }

    ModelBinding {
      tools.compile.adapter may select opencode with model
      anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5 and the effective evaluator invokes only that
      model.

      A Pi evaluator may select model openai/gpt-5 and passes it through one --model
      argument without separating or rewriting its provider prefix.

      An omitted model uses the selected provider CLI default. An empty or non-string
      model is invalid configuration.

      A provider reporting the exact configured model settles normally. A reported
      alias or different model rejects with AGENT_PROVIDER_FAILED; an unreported
      resolved model remains null. With no configured model, any reported resolved
      model is recorded without comparison.
    }

    ProviderEventValidation {
      A completed provider stream with one valid final findings object returns its
      candidate findings, read-only command traces, and mandatory input-token and
      output-token usage.

      Multiple completed assistant messages, multiple JSON documents, or prose
      surrounding the one findings document reject the complete result.

      Tool output and partial assistant text cannot introduce a compiler finding.

      A no-command success returns an empty AgentCommandTrace. Multiple authorized
      operations return one trace entry each in validated terminal-event order.
      Sensitive arguments and output affect applicable budgets but never enter the
      trace.
    }

    CapabilityPolicy {
      A complete prompt containing a surrogate pair at exactly maxInputChars UTF-16
      code units is accepted for invocation; adding one code unit rejects it before
      invocation.

      The operation reaching maxCommands may complete. Observing the next first
      classifiable operation event terminates the provider and rejects every partial
      finding; that read-only operation may already have started or completed.
      Command output and token usage exactly at their limits settle; one unit over
      rejects.

      A zero, negative, fractional, non-finite, nonnumeric, or unsafe-integer limit
      rejects with AGENT_BUDGET_INVALID before provider invocation.

      A target, workspace snapshot, or stage-purpose mismatch with the complete
      PurposeRetrievalResult rejects with AGENT_REQUEST_INVALID before prompt
      rendering or provider invocation.
    }
  }
}
